            <ArticleTitle>[Tl-201 myocardial SPECT in silent myocardial ischemia and angiographically proven coronary heart disease].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>574-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Silent myocardial ischemia is defined as true myocardial ischemia without angina pectoris in patients with angiographically detected coronary artery disease. In this study 52 patients (46 male, 8 female: mean age 53 years) with a pathological exercise test but no symptoms were investigated. They showed stenosis of 75% or more of the diameter in at least one coronary segment on angiography. Prior to or after catheterization (within 14 days) Tl-201 SPECT was done and evaluated independently of angiography. A clear correlation between angiographically confirmed stenosis and reversible perfusion defects with Tl-201 SPECT was established (62 out of 76 lesions). Furthermore, there was a significant relation between angiographically detected subtotal or total occlusions of coronary vessels and irreversible perfusion defects using Tl-201 SPECT (35 in 44 lesions) (p less than 0.001). In patients with ST depression but without angina pectoris during the exercise test, the Tl-201 SPECT is highly suited to determine the hemodynamic effect of coronary stenoses.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
